2494. Merge Overlapping Events in the Same Hall
Table: HallEvents
Column Name	Type
hall_id	int
start_day	date
end_day	date

There is no primary key for this table.

Each row represents an event that takes place in a hall from start_day to end_day (inclusive).

Events in different halls are independent.

Definition

Two events in the same hall are considered overlapping if:

The start_day of one event is less than or equal to
the end_day of the other event.

Overlapping events should be merged into a single event that covers the full time span.

Task

Write an SQL query to merge all overlapping events in the same hall.

For each merged event, report:

hall_id

start_day — the earliest start day of the merged interval

end_day — the latest end day of the merged interval

Rules

Only events in the same hall can be merged.

The merged events should be non-overlapping and sorted by start_day within each hall.

Return the result table in any order.

Example
Input

HallEvents

hall_id	start_day	end_day
1	2022-01-01	2022-01-05
1	2022-01-04	2022-01-08
1	2022-01-10	2022-01-12
2	2022-01-03	2022-01-06
2	2022-01-05	2022-01-07
Output
hall_id	start_day	end_day
1	2022-01-01	2022-01-08
1	2022-01-10	2022-01-12
2	2022-01-03	2022-01-07

Solution
---------
select
    hall_id,
    min(start_day) as start_day,
    max(end_day) as end_day
from (
    select 
        *,
        sum(case when prev_start is null or start_day > prev_end then 1 else 0 end) over(partition by hall_id order by start_day) as grp
    from (
            select
                *,
                lag(start_day) over(partition by hall_id order by start_day) prev_start,
                max(end_day) over(partition by hall_id order by start_day rows between unbounded preceding and 1 preceding) prev_end
            from  HallEvents
        ) a
        order by hall_id, start_day
) b
group by hall_id, grp
